C=7
SRC=['ājñaptāste tato daityāścaṇḍamuṇḍapurogamāḥ | / caturaṅgabalopetā yayurabhyudyatāyudhāḥ || 2||',
'dadṛśuste tato devīmīṣaddhāsāṁ vyavasthitām | / siṁhasyopari śailendraśṛṅge mahati kāñcane || 3||',
'te dṛṣṭvā tāṁ samādātumudyamaṁ cakrurudyatāḥ | / ākṛṣṭacāpāsidharāstathānye tatsamīpagāḥ || 4||',
'tataḥ kopaṁ cakāroccairambikā tānarīnprati | / kopena cāsyā vadanaṁ maṣīvarṇamabhūttadā || 5||',
'bhrukuṭīkuṭilāttasyā lalāṭaphalakāddrutam | / kālī karālavadanā viniṣkrāntāsipāśinī || 6||',
'vicitrakhaṭvāṅgadharā naramālāvibhūṣaṇā | / dvīpicarmaparīdhānā śuṣkamāṁsātibhairavā || 7||',
'ativistāravadanā jihvālalanabhīṣaṇā | / nimagnāraktanayanā nādāpūritadiṅmukhā || 8||',
'sā vegenābhipatitā ghātayantī mahāsurān | / sainye tatra surārīṇāmabhakṣayata tadbalam || 9||',
'pārṣṇigrāhāṅkuśagrāhayodhaghaṇṭāsamanvitān | / samādāyaikahastena mukhe cikṣepa vāraṇān || 10||',
'tathaiva yodhaṁ turagai rathaṁ sārathinā saha | / nikṣipya vaktre daśanaiścarvayantyatibhairavam || 11||',
'ekaṁ jagrāha keśeṣu grīvāyāmatha cāparam | / pādenākramya caivānyamurasānyamapothayat || 12||',
'tairmuktāni ca śastrāṇi mahāstrāṇi tathāsuraiḥ | / mukhena jagrāha ruṣā daśanairmathitānyapi || 13||',
'balināṁ tadbalaṁ sarvamasurāṇāṁ durātmanām | / mamardābhakṣayaccānyānanyāṁścātāḍayattadā || 14||',
'asinā nihatāḥ kecitkecitkhaṭvāṅgatāḍitāḥ | / jagmurvināśamasurā dantāgrābhihatāstathā || 15||',
'kṣaṇena tadbalaṁ sarvamasurāṇāṁ nipātitam | / dṛṣṭvā caṇḍo’bhidudrāva tāṁ kālīmatibhīṣaṇām || 16||',
'śaravarṣairmahābhīmairbhīmākṣīṁ tāṁ mahāsuraḥ | / chādayāmāsa cakraiśca muṇḍaḥ kṣiptaiḥ sahasraśaḥ || 17||',
'tāni cakrāṇyanekāni viśamānāni tanmukham | / babhuryathā’rkabimbāni subahūni ghanodaram || 18||',
'tato jahāsātiruṣā bhīmaṁ bhairavanādinī | / kālī karālavadanā durdarśadaśanojjvalā || 19||',
'utthāya ca mahāsiṁhaṁ devī caṇḍamadhāvata | / gṛhītvā cāsya keśeṣu śirastenāsinācchinat || 20||',
'atha muṇḍo’bhyadhāvattāṁ dṛṣṭvā caṇḍaṁ nipātitam | / tamapyapātayadbhūmau sā khaḍgābhihataṁ ruṣā || 21||',
'hataśeṣaṁ tataḥ sainyaṁ dṛṣṭvā caṇḍaṁ nipātitam | / muṇḍaṁ ca sumahāvīryaṁ diśo bheje bhayāturam || 22||',
'śiraścaṇḍasya kālī ca gṛhītvā muṇḍameva ca | / prāha pracaṇḍāṭṭahāsamiśramabhyetya caṇḍikām || 23||',
'mayā tavātropahṛtau caṇḍamuṇḍau mahāpaśū | / yuddhayajñe svayaṁ śumbhaṁ niśumbhaṁ ca haniṣyasi || 24||',
'tāvānītau tato dṛṣṭvā caṇḍamuṇḍau mahāsurau | / uvāca kālīṁ kalyāṇī lalitaṁ caṇḍikā vacaḥ || 26||',
'yasmāccaṇḍaṁ ca muṇḍaṁ ca gṛhītvā tvamupāgatā | / cāmuṇḍeti tato loke khyātā devī bhaviṣyasi || 27||']
def num(s): return re.findall(r'\|\|\s*(\d+)\s*\|\|',s)
FV=[dict(n=num(s)[0],lines=L(s)) for s in SRC]
def S(*ns): return ' / '.join(s for s in SRC if num(s)[0] in ns)
def KALIF(x,y,s=1):
    return (f'<g transform="translate({x} {y}) scale({s})" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2.2">'
     '<path d="M-24 76 q-30 -6 -42 -30 M24 76 q30 -6 42 -30" fill="none" stroke="#2b2b3a" stroke-width="7"/>'
     '<path d="M-18 30 q-10 40 -4 70 M18 30 q10 40 4 70" stroke="#111" stroke-width="10" fill="none"/>'
     '<circle cy="42" r="15" fill="#2b2b3a"/><circle cx="-5" cy="40" r="3" fill="#e33" stroke="none"/><circle cx="5" cy="40" r="3" fill="#e33" stroke="none"/>'
     '<path d="M-2 50 q2 14 4 0" fill="#e33" stroke="none"/><path d="M-26 150 q-6 -70 26 -88 q32 18 26 88z" fill="#5a4a3a"/>'
     +''.join(f'<circle cx="{-20+k*8}" cy="{70+abs(k-2.5)*3:.0f}" r="4" fill="#eee"/>' for k in range(6))+
     '<path d="M-66 46 v-30" stroke="#ccc" stroke-width="4"/><circle cx="66" cy="40" r="10" fill="#eee"/></g>')
V=[
VP(C,'2–4',S('2','3','4'),'THE SMILING GODDESS ON THE GOLDEN PEAK',
 sc('The Goddess smiling gently on her lion atop a golden mountain peak while an army with bows and swords climbs toward her',HIMA+'<path d="M90 60 L130 20 L170 60z" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+DEVI(130,-10,.45,False)+LION(130,64,.35)+''.join(ASURA(20+k*26,150,.5,'#3a2a6a') for k in range(6))+'<path d="M140 18 q-6 4 -12 0" stroke="#111" fill="none"/>'),
 'Then, commanded, the daityas, led by Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa, marched with the fourfold army, weapons raised. They saw the Devī, gently smiling, seated on her lion on a great golden peak of the king of mountains. Seeing her, they made ready to seize her; some drew their bows, others held swords, and came near her.',
 [('caturaṅga-bala','<b>the fourfold army</b>: chariots, elephants, horses, foot'),('īṣat-hāsām','<b>gently smiling</b>'),('śaila-indra','<b>the king of mountains</b>'),('kāñcana','<b>golden</b>'),('ākṛṣṭa-cāpa','<b>with bows drawn</b>')],
 '<b>Still smiling.</b> An army comes to drag her away, and she is smiling. The calm before the storm: in two verses her face will turn black as ink.',
 'An army comes; <b>she smiles</b>.'),
VP(C,'5–6',S('5','6'),'KĀLĪ IS BORN',
 sc('The Goddess’s face darkening with anger, and from her frowning forehead a fierce dark goddess with sword and noose leaping out',GLOW(110,60,60,'#2b2140',.5)+'<circle cx="110" cy="50" r="30" fill="#2b2b3a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M92 38 l12 6 M128 38 l-12 6" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="3"/>'+'<path d="M110 22 q0 -16 0 -20" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="4"/>'+KALIF(110,40,.7)),
 'Then Ambikā grew fiercely angry with those enemies, and with her anger her face became black as ink. From her forehead, twisted with frowning brows, swiftly came forth Kālī, of terrible face, armed with sword and noose.',
 [('maṣī-varṇam','<b>the colour of ink</b>'),('bhrukuṭī-kuṭila','<b>twisted with frowns</b>'),('lalāṭa-phalaka','<b>the plane of the forehead</b>'),('kālī','<b>the dark one</b>; also “Time”'),('asi-pāśinī','<b>bearing sword and noose</b>')],
 '<b>The birth of Kālī.</b> This is one of the most famous moments in Hindu scripture. Kālī is not a separate goddess called for help: she is the Goddess’s own anger, leaping out of her brow. The fiercest form comes from the one who was smiling a moment ago.',
 'Kālī is <b>the Goddess’s own fury, made visible</b>.'),
VP(C,'7–8',S('7','8'),'HOW KĀLĪ LOOKS',
 sc('Kali close up: skull-topped staff, garland of heads, tiger-skin garment, gaunt body, wide mouth with lolling tongue, sunken red eyes',KALIF(110,0,1.0)+T(30,40,'khaṭvāṅga',9)+T(190,40,'tongue',9)+T(30,130,'tiger skin',9)+T(190,110,'garland',9)),
 'She carried a strange skull-topped staff, wore a garland of human heads, was clad in a tiger’s skin, terrible with her withered flesh; her mouth gaping wide, fearsome with her lolling tongue, her eyes sunken and red, she filled the directions with her roar.',
 [('khaṭvāṅga','<b>a skull-topped staff</b>'),('nara-mālā','<b>a garland of human heads</b>'),('dvīpi-carman','<b>a tiger’s (leopard’s) skin</b>'),('śuṣka-māṃsa','<b>withered flesh</b>'),('jihvā-lalana','<b>lolling tongue</b>'),('nimagna-ārakta-nayanā','<b>sunken red eyes</b>')],
 '<b>The source of every Kālī image.</b> The skull staff, the garland of heads, the lolling tongue: every later painting and idol of Kālī goes back to these two verses. Her look is meant to terrify the ego, not the devotee.',
 'Every image of Kālī <b>comes from these lines</b>.'),
VP(C,'9–11',S('9','10','11'),'SHE SWALLOWS ELEPHANTS',
 sc('Gigantic Kali scooping up elephants with their riders and bells in one hand and throwing them into her mouth, chariots following',BATTLE+KALIF(130,-10,1.0)+ELEPHANT(60,140,.7)+ELEPHANT(170,40,.4)+CHARIOT(40,100,.5)+'<path d="M150 50 q-6 -10 6 -14" stroke="#111" fill="none"/>'),
 'Rushing in with great speed, slaughtering the great asuras, she devoured that army of the gods’ enemies. Seizing elephants in one hand, with their rear-guards, goad-bearers, warriors and bells, she threw them into her mouth. Likewise the warrior with his horses, the chariot with its charioteer, she put into her mouth and chewed them most terribly with her teeth.',
 [('abhakṣayata','<b>devoured</b>'),('vāraṇa','<b>elephants</b>'),('aṅkuśa-grāha','<b>goad-bearers</b>, mahouts'),('carvayantī','<b>chewing</b>'),('ati-bhairavam','<b>most terribly</b>')],
 '<b>Kālī eats.</b> Her hunger has no end: elephants, horses, chariots, the whole army. Read symbolically, Kālī is Time (kāla) devouring everything; and the power that devours our pride and illusions whole.',
 'Kālī, as Time, <b>devours everything</b>.'),
VP(C,'12–13',S('12','13'),'WEAPONS IN HER TEETH',
 sc('Kali seizing one asura by the hair, another by the neck, trampling a third, and catching flying weapons in her teeth',BATTLE+KALIF(110,-6,.9)+ASURA(40,90,.6)+ASURA(180,90,.6,'#3a2a6a')+ASURA(110,156,.5)+ARROWS(60,40,4)+'<path d="M106 40 l8 0" stroke="#999" stroke-width="4"/>'),
 'One she seized by the hair, another by the neck; one she crushed underfoot, another she smashed with her chest. The weapons and great missiles hurled by the asuras she caught in her mouth in fury and crushed with her teeth.',
 [('keśeṣu','<b>by the hair</b>'),('grīvāyām','<b>by the neck</b>'),('pādena ākramya','<b>trampling with her foot</b>'),('mahā-astra','<b>great missiles</b>'),('daśanaiḥ mathitāni','<b>crushed by her teeth</b>')],
 '<b>Poetic justice.</b> The asuras came to drag the Goddess “by the hair”. Kālī seizes <i>them</i> by the hair. Their weapons do not wound her; she eats them.',
 'They came to drag her by the hair; <b>Kālī drags them</b>.'),
VP(C,'16–18',S('16','17','18'),'SUNS INTO A CLOUD',
 sc('Munda hurling hundreds of glowing discuses into Kali’s mouth, where they disappear like many suns sinking into a dark cloud',NIGHTSKY+'<ellipse cx="140" cy="70" rx="56" ry="40" fill="#1c1430" stroke="#555"/>'+''.join(f'<circle cx="{40+k*14}" cy="{100-k*6}" r="8" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#e0a800" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(7))+ASURA(30,150,.8,'#2a2a7a')+T(150,140,'like suns into a cloud',9,'#fff')),
 'Seeing the whole asura army fallen in a moment, Caṇḍa rushed at that most terrible Kālī. The great asura covered the terrible-eyed one with showers of fearsome arrows, and Muṇḍa with discuses hurled by the thousand. Those many discuses, entering her mouth, looked like many suns’ discs entering the belly of a cloud.',
 [('abhidudrāva','<b>rushed at</b>'),('bhīma-akṣī','<b>terrible-eyed</b>'),('cakra','<b>discus</b>'),('viśamānāni','<b>entering</b>'),('arka-bimba','<b>disc of the sun</b>'),('ghana-udaram','<b>the belly of a cloud</b>')],
 '<b>A great image.</b> Blazing discuses vanish into Kālī’s mouth “like suns into a dark cloud”. Kālī is the dark cloud that swallows all light and all weapons: nothing aimed at her survives.',
 'Their weapons vanish in her <b>like suns in a cloud</b>.'),
VP(C,'19–21',S('19','20','21'),'CAṆḌA AND MUṆḌA FALL',
 sc('Kali laughing terribly, leaping on the great lion, seizing Chanda by the hair and swinging her sword; Munda falling',BATTLE+LION(110,130,1.0)+KALIF(110,-14,.75)+SWORD(160,50,40,1)+ASURA(180,110,.7,'#7a2a2a')+ASURA(40,150,.6,'#2a2a7a')+T(60,30,'HA HA HA!',13,'#c0392b')),
 'Then Kālī, roaring terribly, with terrible face and gleaming fearsome teeth, laughed a fearful laugh in her fury. Mounting her great lion, the Devī rushed at Caṇḍa, seized him by the hair, and cut off his head with her sword. Then Muṇḍa, seeing Caṇḍa fallen, rushed at her; she struck him down too, in fury, with her sword.',
 [('jahāsa','<b>laughed</b>'),('bhairava-nādinī','<b>roaring terribly</b>'),('durdarśa-daśana','<b>teeth fearful to see</b>'),('utthāya mahā-siṃham','<b>mounting the great lion</b>'),('khaḍga-abhihatam','<b>struck by the sword</b>')],
 '<b>The laugh again.</b> Like the Goddess in chapter 2, Kālī laughs before she strikes. Note that she rides the lion here: Kālī and the Devī are one, and share everything.',
 'Kālī laughs, and <b>the two generals fall</b>.'),
VP(C,'23–24',S('23','24'),'“TWO GREAT BEASTS FOR YOUR SACRIFICE”',
 sc('Kali holding two severed heads out to the radiant Goddess, laughing, beside a sacrificial fire',FIRE(110,130,.8)+KALIF(56,0,.6)+DEVI(170,0,.55,False)+'<circle cx="96" cy="60" r="10" fill="#7a2a2a" stroke="#111"/><circle cx="96" cy="84" r="10" fill="#2a2a7a" stroke="#111"/>'),
 'Taking the heads of Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa, Kālī came to Caṇḍikā and said, with a fierce, loud laugh: “Here I bring you Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa, two great beasts. In the sacrifice of battle you yourself will slay Śumbha and Niśumbha.”',
 [('upahṛtau','<b>offered, brought</b>'),('mahā-paśū','<b>two great beasts</b>, sacrificial animals'),('yuddha-yajña','<b>the sacrifice of battle</b>'),('svayam','<b>yourself</b>')],
 '<b>War as a yajña.</b> Kālī calls the battle a sacrifice and the generals its victims. She leaves the final offering, Śumbha and Niśumbha, for the Goddess herself.',
 'Kālī offers the battle <b>as a sacrifice</b>.'),
VP(C,'26–27',S('26','27'),'THE NAME CĀMUṆḌĀ',
 sc('The radiant Goddess gently placing a garland on Kali, the name Chamunda written above',GLOW(110,40,50)+DEVI(160,0,.6,False)+KALIF(60,10,.6)+'<path d="M80 50 q20 -20 50 -6" fill="none" stroke="#e05a8a" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="2 4"/>'+T(110,152,'CĀMUṆḌĀ',18,'#c0392b')),
 'Seeing the two great asuras Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa brought before her, the gracious Caṇḍikā spoke to Kālī these gentle words: “Because you have come bringing Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa, you, O Devī, will be famed in the world as Cāmuṇḍā.”',
 [('kalyāṇī','<b>the gracious one</b>'),('lalitam vacaḥ','<b>gentle, playful words</b>'),('cāmuṇḍā','<b>Caṇḍa + Muṇḍa</b>: the slayer of both'),('khyātā','<b>famed</b>')],
 '<b>A name is given.</b> From this moment Kālī is <b>Cāmuṇḍā</b>, a name honoured in temples across India (Chamundeshwari on Chamundi Hill in Mysuru among them). Note the tone: Caṇḍikā speaks “gently”, like a mother naming a child.',
 'From this day, Kālī is <b>Cāmuṇḍā</b>.'),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="Dark Kali leaping out of the frowning forehead of the Goddess, garland of heads, tongue lolling, sword raised, over the asura army">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#1c0a0a" rx="8"/>'+GLOW(190,70,90,'#c0392b',.3)+DEVI(300,30,.6,False)+'<path d="M290 50 Q240 40 200 80" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="4" fill="none"/>'+KALIF(170,40,1.15)+SWORD(240,150,30,1.4)+''.join(ASURA(20+k*40,262,.6,'#3a2a6a') for k in range(4))+ELEPHANT(320,240,.7)+'</svg>')
st=storypage('kali','THE DARK ONE','Ślokas 7.1–27',
 panels('Three panels: the Goddess smiling on the golden peak; Kali leaping from her forehead; Kali bringing the two heads',[
  ('#fff3b0','SHE SMILES',DEVI(72,10,.55,False)+LION(72,130,.45)),
  ('#2b2140','KĀLĪ!',KALIF(72,10,.6)),
  ('#fde4e4','CĀMUṆḌĀ',KALIF(40,30,.45)+DEVI(110,20,.4,False)+'<circle cx="72" cy="80" r="7" fill="#7a2a2a" stroke="#111"/><circle cx="72" cy="98" r="7" fill="#2a2a7a" stroke="#111"/>')]),
 ['<b>The army.</b> Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa march with the fourfold army and find her smiling on a golden peak.',
  '<b>Anger.</b> As they draw bows and swords, her face turns black as ink, and from her forehead leaps <b>Kālī</b>, with sword and noose, skull staff and garland of heads.',
  '<b>The feast.</b> Kālī devours elephants, horses, chariots and warriors; catches weapons in her teeth; swallows Muṇḍa’s discuses like a cloud swallowing suns.',
  '<b>The generals.</b> Laughing, she mounts the lion, beheads Caṇḍa, then Muṇḍa. The rest of the army flees.',
  '<b>The name.</b> She brings the two heads to Caṇḍikā: “Two beasts for the sacrifice of battle.” Caṇḍikā names her <b>Cāmuṇḍā</b>.'],
 ("Kālī is so scary. Why do people love her?","Because she is scary only to what harms us. To devotees, Kālī is the fiercest Mother: the one who eats our fears whole. Bengal’s great poets call her simply 'Mā'."),
 'The fiercest form is <b>the Mother’s protection</b>.')
P=dict(n=12,ch=7,out='/home/claude/work/devim/Devi_Mahatmya_Part12.html',
 title='Caṇḍa, Muṇḍa and Cāmuṇḍā · Part 12 · Devī Māhātmya series · srini.presents',
 desc='Devī Māhātmya, Part 12: chapter 7. Kālī leaps from the Goddess’s frowning forehead, devours the asura army, slays Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa and is named Cāmuṇḍā. Explained in plain English with illustrations.',
 h1='CĀMUṆḌĀ',series='DEVĪ MĀHĀTMYA · PART 12 · CHAPTER 7',tag='Kālī leaps from the brow of the Goddess · explained and illustrated',cover=cover,
 how='The whole of chapter 7 <b>in full for chanting</b>, the story in <b>comic panels</b>, and the key ślokas on their own pages: the chant, a picture, what it says, the key words, one insight and the one thing to remember.',
 where=[f'In {plink(11)}, the Goddess turned Dhūmralocana to ash with one “huṃ”. Śumbha, lip trembling, sent his two great generals, <b>Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa</b>, with a bigger army.',
  'Chapter 7 gives us the birth of <b>Kālī</b> from the Goddess’s own anger, and her famous name, <b>Cāmuṇḍā</b>. For a Mysuru reader this chapter is close to home: Chamundi Hill is named after her.'],
 wtalk=("Is Kālī a different goddess?","The text says she leaps out of the Goddess's own forehead. Same Goddess, different face: the anger of a mother whose children are threatened."),
 fulls=[('CHAPTER 7 IN ONE GO','Ślokas 2–14',FV[:13]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Ślokas 15–27',FV[13:])],
 fulltoc='Chapter 7 in One Go',
 fullnote='Text as given in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in (27 ślokas in chapter 7; speaker lines omitted); its numbering is followed.',
 seq=[st]+V,
 toc=[('THE STORY',[('kali','The Dark One')]),('THE KEY VERSES',V)],
 end=['<b>The smile (7.2–4).</b> Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa’s army finds her smiling on a golden peak and moves to seize her.',
  '<b>Kālī (7.5–8).</b> Her face turns black with anger; Kālī leaps from her brow: sword, noose, skull staff, garland of heads, lolling tongue.',
  '<b>The feast (7.9–15).</b> Kālī devours elephants, chariots and warriors, catches weapons in her teeth.',
  '<b>The generals (7.16–22).</b> Discuses vanish in her like suns in a cloud; laughing, she beheads Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa.',
  '<b>Cāmuṇḍā (7.23–27).</b> She offers the heads “for the sacrifice of battle”; Caṇḍikā names her Cāmuṇḍā.'],
 endsub='Chapter 7 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",'Part 13: chapter 8. The Seven Mothers come out of the gods to fight, and the demon Raktabīja appears: every drop of his blood that touches the earth becomes another Raktabīja.'),
 endkey='<b>the Mother’s anger is Kālī, and Kālī is Cāmuṇḍā</b>.',
 foot='Study notes on the Devī Māhātmya, chapter 7 (Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa). The chapter is given in full as in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in, whose numbering is followed. The reading of Kālī as Time is traditional interpretation. Meanings are our own plain-English renderings. Zappy and Prof. Bolt are fictional.')
